/// The `callback` interface provides a means of being reached from outside the
/// host application: an address the host sets aside for the caller, and the
/// delivery of a single URI directed at it.
///
/// Nothing here causes a URI to be sent. Arranging that is the caller's
/// business — handing the address to a service that is to return to it, or to
/// a person who will follow a link.
///
/// A host may have no way of being reached from outside itself. That is not
/// exceptional, and callers are expected to carry on without.
interface callback {
    use wasi:clocks/monotonic-clock@0.2.7.{duration};
    use wasi:io/poll@0.2.7.{pollable};

    /// Why a listener could not be established.
    variant listen-error {
        /// This host cannot be reached from outside itself, so no address it
        /// set aside would be of any use. This is a standing property of the
        /// host rather than a fault, and a caller that needs to be reached
        /// should turn to some other means.
        unsupported,

        /// The host failed to listen, for a reason described by the payload.
        /// The string is meant for diagnostics; its contents are unspecified
        /// and callers must not depend on them.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why nothing was delivered.
    variant receive-error {
        /// The timeout elapsed with nothing delivered.
        expired,

        /// Whoever was to deliver gave up, for a reason described by the
        /// payload. The string is meant for diagnostics; its contents are
        /// unspecified and callers must not depend on them.
        other(string),
    }

    /// An address set aside for the caller, and the means of receiving a
    /// single URI delivered to it.
    ///
    /// The host decides whether the address is stable or variable. It may
    /// expose a stable, pre-registerable address scoped to the caller, or
    /// issue a different address for each listener. A caller cannot choose or
    /// influence the address it is given, and must not assume that it can be
    /// reused after the listener is dropped unless the host documents that
    /// guarantee.
    ///
    /// A listener is listening from the moment it exists, which is before its
    /// address can be read. A URI may be delivered the moment the address is
    /// known to anyone else, and a listener established after the fact would
    /// miss it.
    resource listener {
        /// Sets aside an address and begins listening.
        ///
        /// * `timeout`: How long to listen before giving up with
        ///   `receive-error::expired`.
        ///
        /// Returns a listener, or the reason one could not be established.
        listen: static func(timeout: duration) -> result<listener, listen-error>;

        /// Returns the absolute URI that must be used to reach this
        /// listener.
        ///
        /// The returned string is a syntactically valid absolute URI. It does
        /// not change for as long as the listener exists, and this method may
        /// be called as often as the caller likes, including after the listener
        /// has become ready.
        address: func() -> string;

        /// Returns a pollable which becomes ready once a URI has been
        /// delivered or the listener has given up. May be called more than
        /// once; every pollable so returned becomes ready at the same moment.
        ///
        /// The pollable can be passed to `wasi:io/poll.poll` together with other
        /// pollables, allowing other events to be observed without abandoning
        /// the wait for a callback.
        ///
        /// The returned `pollable` is a child resource of this `listener` and
        /// must be dropped before it. Dropping the `listener` while a child
        /// pollable is still alive traps.
        subscribe: func() -> pollable;

        /// Returns the URI that was delivered, once the listener is ready.
        ///
        /// The outer `option` stands for readiness; callers can wait on it
        /// becoming `some` using `subscribe`. The outer `result` allows the
        /// value to be taken at most once: it is success on the first call in
        /// which the outer option is `some`, and an error on every call after
        /// that.
        ///
        /// The inner `result` represents either the URI that was delivered or
        /// the reason nothing was delivered.
        ///
        /// The URI is returned whole, with everything it carried, including
        /// any fragment. A fragment is not transmitted when a URI is fetched
        /// over a network protocol, so a host that listens by serving its
        /// address must recover the fragment by some other means; callers may
        /// count on it being present.
        ///
        /// A listener takes delivery at most once. Further deliveries are not
        /// delivered through this listener.
        get: func() -> option<result<result<string, receive-error>>>;
    }
}
